# Ru Species Supported on MOF‐Derived N‐Doped TiO<sub>2</sub>/C Hybrids as Efficient Electrocatalytic/Photocatalytic Hydrogen Evolution Reaction Catalysts.

## abstract
The development of efficient catalysts is of great importance for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) of water splitting via electrocatalytic/photocatalytic processes to remediate the current severe environmental and energy problems. By aid of the stabilization effects of uncoordinated groups and inherent pore‐confinement of amine‐functionalized metal–organic frameworks (NH2‐MIL‐125), two forms of Ru species including nanoparticles (NPs) and/or single atoms (SAs) can be firmly embedded in NH2‐MIL‐125 derived N‐doped TiO2/C support (N‐TC), and thus obtain two kinds of samples named Ru‐NPs/SAs@N‐TC and Ru‐SAs@N‐TC, respectively. In the synthetic process, the initial feeding amount of Ru3  ions not only strongly determines the final size and dispersion states of Ru species but also the morphology and defective structures of N‐TC support. Impressively, Ru‐NPs/SAs@N‐TC exhibit superior catalytic activities to Ru‐SAs@N‐TC for either electrocatalytic or photocatalytic HER. This should be attributed to its larger specific surface area and benefiting from synergistic coupling of Ru NPs and Ru SAs. It is envisioned that the present work can provide a new avenue for development of high‐efficiency and multifunctional hybrid catalysts in sustainable energy conversion.

Ru Species Supported on MOF-Derived N-Doped TiO2/C Hybrids as Efficient Electrocatalytic/Photocatalytic Hydrogen Evolution Reaction Catalysts
The development of efficient catalysts is of great importance for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) of water splitting via electrocatalytic/photocatalytic processes to remediate the current severe environmental and energy problems. By aid of the stabilization effects of uncoordinated groups and inherent pore-confinement of amine-functionalized metal–organic frameworks $(N H_{2}-$ MIL-125), two forms of Ru species including nanoparticles (NPs) and/or single atoms (SAs) can be firmly embedded in $N H_{2}$ -MIL-125 derived N-doped $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ support (N-TC), and thus obtain two kinds of samples named $R u-N P s/$ SAs@N-TC and Ru-SAs@N-TC, respectively. In the synthetic process, the initial feeding amount of $\mathsf{R u}^{3+}$ ions not only strongly determines the final size and dispersion states of Ru species but also the morphology and defective structures of N-TC support. Impressively, Ru-NPs/SAs@N-TC exhibit superior catalytic activities to $R u-S A s@N-T C$ for either electrocatalytic or photocatalytic HER. This should be attributed to its larger specific surface area and benefiting from synergistic coupling of $R\cup N P s$ and $\mathsf{R}\mathsf{u}$ SAs. It is envisioned that the present work can provide a new avenue for development of high-efficiency and multifunctional hybrid catalysts in sustainable energy conversion.
## 1. Introduction
Hydrogen production via catalysis has been considered as a promising and green technology to provide sustainable energy carriers and address the urgent environmental issues.[1] Wherein, the pivotal challenge is to develop more efficient hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) catalysts to generate hydrogen via electrochemical or solar-energy-driven water splitting in the blueprint for green energy utilization.[2] Despite electrocatalytic and photocatalytic HER followed relatively different mechanisms, some main components of electrocatalysts often overlap with many cocatalysts, while for photocatalysts.[3] Hence, advanced catalysts should be highly desired in combination of electrocatalytic and photocatalytic HER activities. For example, Pt-based nanomaterials have been generally used as electrocatalysts for water splitting, and the single Pt component was not simultaneously responsive to optical and electric energy, but they could be served as ideal cocatalysts in photocatalytic redox reactions to boost charge transfer and separation.[4] Considering the high cost and low reserve of Pt catalysts,[5] it must be a big challenge to explore efficient hybrid catalysts by replacement of Pt in both electrocatalytic and photocatalytic HER processes for practical applications.
As the cheapest platinum group metal, Ru has been alternatively thought to be the most promising candidate for Pt-based catalysts because of the close bond energy of $\mathrm{\Ru{-}H}$ to that of PtH.[6] Plenty of successful cases have identified the activity of Ru catalysts in electrocatalytic HER, and some even performed well over a wide range of pH values.[7] Nevertheless, the utilization efficiency of Ru seems still not that satisfactory in light of its intrinsic activity, which is restricted by the relatively large particle size, poor size distribution, and high loading amount.[8] As reported, if well-dispersed metal nanoparticles (NPs) and single atoms (SAs) coexisted on the support, they could show greatly enhanced mass activity, benefiting from the optimized electronic structures of SAs by their adjacent metal NPs.[9] For instance, the catalysts integrated of Fe NPs with Fe SAs resulted in markedly enhanced oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) performance.[10] Exceptionally high activity could be obtained in electrocatalytic HER, if Pt NPs were anchored along with Pt SAs on functionalized multiwall carbon nanotubes.[11] The coexistence of high-content $\mathrm{Fe-N}_{x}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ NPs was essential for achieving high ORR activity.[12] Also, $\mathrm{{Ru/Co}}$ supported on N-doped carbon showed an outstanding performance on electrocatalytic HER caused by synergistic coupling between Ru NPs and Co SAs.[13] These successful cases accordantly point out that it should be a meaningful way to modulate the electronic structures of single atomic centers by neighboring other metal NPs and thus to achieve their optimal intrinsic catalytic activities.
As reported, it has been identified as feasible to precisely manipulate the components and dispersion states of catalysts by utilizing the versatile and structure-tailorable metal–organic frameworks (MOFs).[14] Diversified inorganic metal-containing nodes and organic linkers endow MOFs with rich coordination sites and defects that could help implant individual active metal species and prevent them from aggregation during pyrolysis.[15] Especially for amine-functionalized MOFs, the free amine groups $\left(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right)$ efficiently stabilized metal cations via strong coordination between its lone pair electrons and empty d-orbitals of transiton metal atoms.[16] Given the well-defined pores and inherent large specific surface areas, MOFs could also afford rich internal voids to hold guests in the form of SAs, clusters, and $\mathrm{NPs.^{[17]}}$ Besides, the final states of embedded metal species were strongly determined by coordination/confinement of the amine-functionalized MOF structures.
Herein, we utilized the precursor of $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}$ -incorporated $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL-125 (dubbed as $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125), which was obtained by introduction of a certain amount of $\mathrm{RuCl}_{3}$ during the very beginning growth of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125, to prepare Ru/N-TC catalysts via a facile pyrolysis process. The dispersion states of Ru species were able to be indirectly regulated just by varying the initial feeding amount of $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}$ ions. As a result, the Ru NPs and SAs coexisted sample of Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC (derived from $2\%$ $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125) exhibited much higher activity than that of Ru-SAs $@$ N-TC (derived from $5\%$ $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125) either in electrocatalytic or photocatalytic HER in agreement with the proposed synergistic coupling effect. Scheme  1 demonstrates the whole synthetic strategy for preparation of $\mathrm{{Ru-NPs}/}$ SAs $@$ N-TC and Ru-SAs $@$ N-TC samples.
## 2. Results and Discussion
As shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information), the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 with the feeding ratio of $\mathrm{{Ru/Ti}}$ at $\mathbf{x\%}$ ( $2\%$ and $5\%$ ) displayed characteristic peaks of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125$ . However, the crystallinity of MOFs was decreased with the increase ratio of $\mathrm{{Ru/Ti}}$ , indicating the strong interference of $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}$ ions on $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 growth.[18] The Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information) proved the coordination interaction between $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}$ ions and $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups. Two peaks at $1666.3\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (rocking vibration) and $1095.4~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (scissoring vibration) should be ascribed to the characteristic signals of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125.[19] Especially for $5\%\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125, these two peaks seem to be much stronger because of the existence of more $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}$ ions anchored by $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ groups in comparison with $2\%\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125. After pyrolysis at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 was completely transformed into N-TC that are mainly composed by $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as proved by the XRD patterns (Figure S3, Supporting Information), and meanwhile there should be left numbers of Ru species embedded in N-TC. Unexpectedly, even if Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC reached to a peak value, the actual content of Ru was just $0.457~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $0.360\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ for ${\mathrm{Ru-NPs}}/{\mathrm{SAs}}\ @{\mathrm{N-TC}}$ and Ru-SAs@N-TC, respectively, far less than its corresponding ratio of $2\%$ and $5\%$ (Figure S4 and Table S1, Supporting Information).
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and scanning electron microscope (SEM) images (Figure  1; and Figure S5, Supporting Information) reveals that Ru-NPs/SAs@N-TC well maintained the starting tablet-like morphology of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ MIL-125, however, Ru-SAs@N-TC was composed by irregular fragments. This different morphology is inherited from their corresponding precursors, demonstrating that the incorporation of $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}$ ions have disturbed the growth of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 and further affected the structures of pyrolysis products. TEM images show that Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC is mainly composed by $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs in a size of ${\approx}20~\mathrm{nm}$ , and in its high-resolution TEM (HR-TEM) image (Figure S6, Supporting Information) it is found that the lattice spacings of 0.352 and $0.325~\mathrm{nm}$ could be ascribed to the (101) plane of anatase and (110) crystal plane of rutile phase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , respectively. The conclusion of coexistence of anatase and rutile phase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ has been well supported by the XRD patterns (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Further observation indicates that numerous ultrasmall Ru NPs are evenly distributed on the surface of Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC, but hard seen in the case of ${\mathrm{Ru-SAs@N-TC}}$ . In the high-angle annular dark field-scanning TEM (HAADF-STEM) images (Figure  1c,f), isolated Ru SAs marked by red cycles could be clearly distinguished from the support for both samples, but Ru NPs in yellow just be found in Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC. These Ru NPs are monodispersed on the surface of Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC, and their measured lattice fringe of $0.232\mathrm{nm}$ corresponds well to the (100) facet of ${\mathrm{Ru}}^{0}$ with a hexagonal close-packed crystal structure (Figure S7, Supporting Information). Additionally, the elemental mapping analysis reveals that the elements of Ru, Ti, O, C, and N are all uniformly distributed over both of the two samples (Figure S8, Supporting Information). These results have provided powerful proofs to indicate the initial interactions between $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}$ ions and MOFs have strong influence on the final dispersion states of Ru species.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was then carried out to investigate the surface elemental compositions and chemical states of pure N-TC, Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC, and Ru-SAs $@$ N-TC. As seen in Figure S9 (Supporting Information), Ru signals are hard to be discerned in Ru-NPs/SAs@N-TC and Ru-SAs $@$ N-TC in light of their ultralow Ru content and more importantly the partial overlap of deconvoluted Ti 2p peaks with Ru $3\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ ones.[20] However, upon introduction of Ru species, the binding energies of Ti 2p and O 1s redshifted slightly in sync (Figure  2a,b), suggesting the electronic effect arising from Ru species increased the electron density around Ti sites.[21] The shifts in binding energy should be caused by charge rearrangement and introduction of low valance ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{3+}$ ions or oxygen vacancies $(\mathrm{O}_{\nu})$ in $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ bulk,[22] and the binding environment in the samples became more clearer, if combined with the electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectra. As shown in Figure 2d, a set of intensive EPR signals at $\mathrm{g}=2.003$ and $\mathrm{g}=1.945$ related respectively to $\mathrm{~O}_{\nu}$ and associated $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ ions could be clearly observed for Ru-NPs/SAs@N-TC and $\mathrm{Ru-SAs@N-TC,\ell^{2}}$ 3] but absent from N-TC. Undoubtedly, the much stronger signal at $\mathrm{g}=2.003$ of Ru-SAs $@$ N-TC indicates that the atomically dispersed Ru species must favor creating more $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}{\cdot}0_{\nu}$ defective sites compared to $\mathrm{{Ru}\ N P s}$ These $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{\nu}$ defects could hence provide more trapping sites to boost separation and transform of electrons/holes pairs.[24]
The C 1s XPS spectra (Figure S10, Supporting Information) of the three samples show that the predominant bonds are $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ (284.7 eV), CN (285.7 eV), $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ $(286.8\mathrm{eV})$ , and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{O-C=O}$ $(289.0~\mathrm{eV})$ . It indicates that the occurrence of a graphitization process during pyrolysis, and the C species has been doped by N. As to the high-resolution N 1s XPS spectra (Figure 2c), the peaks located at around 404.0 and $401.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ could be assigned to the oxidized and graphitic N, respectively, and the other peaks at 398.5 and $396.4\mathrm{eV}$ to the interstitial and substitutional N dopants $(\mathrm{Ti-N}$ bond) in the lattice of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , respectively, unveiling the fact of N-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ support.[25] The related data of the relative content and binding energy of $\mathrm{~N~}$ 1s have been listed in Table S2 (Supporting Information). By comparing Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC with other samples, it is found the content of substitutional $\mathrm{~N~}$ was in a relatively low value, however, the ratio of oxidized N presents an obvious enhancement. This result further suggests that the substituting $\mathrm{~N~}$ heteroatoms in the lattice of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ should be dominantly induced by single atomic $\mathtt{R u}$ sites rather than Ru NPs, and thus retained more $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{\nu}$ defects in Ru-SAs $@$ N-TC.
As previously reported, a considerable number of structural defects would be introduced into the carbon substance after doping N or other metal species.[26] Hence, it is reasonably believed that the increased content of oxidized N in $\mathrm{Ru-NPs}/$ S $\scriptstyle\mathrm{is}\ @\mathrm{N-TC}$ should originate from the destruction of N-doped carbon induced by more structural defects. Raman tests (Figure 3a) have revealed the structural defects of the samples. The peak located at $1349~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is related to the D-band, which often appeared in the typically defective or disordered carbon systems, and the in-plane vibrations of graphitic structures should be ascribed to the G-band at the peak of $1590~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ . In general, a high ratio in intensity between D and G bands $\left({I_{\mathrm{D}}}/{I_{\mathrm{G}}}\right)$ could reflect abundant defects/disorders in carbon materials. The calculated values of $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ are 0.92, 0.99, and $0.94$ for N-TC, ${\mathrm{Ru-NPs}}/{\mathrm{SAs}}\ @{\mathrm{N-TC}}$ , and $R\mathbf{u}.\mathbf{S}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}@\mathbf{N}{\cdot}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{C}$ , respectively. Since $\mathtt{R u}$ species do cause the change of surface energy of carbon materials,[27] in our case the distinct structural defects or microstructural rearrangement of atoms should be brought about by the foreign $\mathtt{R u}$ species. Not only that, the higher-concentration defects have induced partial decomposition of the carbon skeleton and decrease of total N contents in $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{Ru-NPs}}/$ SAs $@$ N-TC (Table S2, Supporting Information). And the ample defective sites did help modulate electronic and surface structures, thereby optimizing adsorption energies of catalytic process.[28] The other two peaks at 156 and $612\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ should be related to the anatase phase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and those centered at 248 and $432\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to its rutile phase.
Considering the multiple variations in structures and components between Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC and ${\mathrm{Ru-SAs@N-T(}}$ C, it is indispensable to unveil the pyrolysis process of their corresponding precursors. As presented in Figure S11 (Supporting Information) of the thermal gravimetric and differential scanning calorimetry (TG-DSC) curves, $2\%$ and $5\%\mathrm{\Ru}^{3+}/$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 precursors exhibit remarkably different thermal behaviors compared to pure ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125$ . The higher content of adsorbed $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}$ ions in $2\%\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–125$ is conducive to the more complete thermal pyrolysis, evidenced by its greatest weight loss of $86.4~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . In addition, the formation of pore structure strongly depends on their thermal behaviors of MOFs skeletons during pyrolysis. The $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption curves in Figure 3b demonstrate all the samples shows the typical type IV isotherms with a sharp capillary condensation step at $\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}=0.6–0.7$ and average pore size within $2{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure  3b, inset), indicating their mesoporous features of MOFs-derived products. The detailed data on the specific surface area, pore volume, and pore diameter of all the samples have been listed in Table S3 (Supporting Information). Notably, Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC maintains the highest Brunauer–Emmet– Teller (BET) specific surface area of $307.5\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and the largest pore volume of $0.19\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ among the pyrolysis products, which are beneficial for providing more exposed active sites to boost mass transport.[29] However, the results appeared totally unexpected, because its corresponding precursor of $2\%$ $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 has a specific surface area of $726.1\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ far less than $1504.6~\mathrm{cm}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of pure $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Figure S12, Supporting Information). This result further identifies the coordination/confinement of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125$ toward $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}$ ions could help form abundant pores during the pyrolysis process.
The electrocatalytic HER activities of ${\mathrm{Ru-NPs}}/{\mathrm{SAs}}{\ @\mathrm{N-TC}}$ and Ru-SAs@N-TC catalysts were investigated by a three-electrode electrochemical configuration in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ -saturated $1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}$ solution. Pure N-TC and commercially available $20\%\:\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalysts were used as reference under identical test conditions. As shown in Figure 4a, the onset overpotentials of the two Rucontaining catalysts are both close to $14~\mathrm{mV}$ that is only $10~\mathrm{mV}$ larger than $20\%$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $(4~\mathrm{mV})$ , however, pure N-TC exhibits negligible activity in the whole test potentials. It indicates that electrocatalytic HER activities are mainly contributed by Ru species. The overpotentials at a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ are 97 and $159\mathrm{mV}$ for Ru-NPs/SAs $@.$ N-TC and Ru-SAs $@$ N-TC, respectively. In addition, the Tafel slope of Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC is $58\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , which is close to $45~\mathrm{mV}$ dec−1 of $20\%\:\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst but lower than $75~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ of Ru-SAs $@$ N-TC (Figure 4b). Obviously, Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC shows faster reaction kinetics in $1\mathrm{~\bf~{~M~}~}$ KOH solution, and thus a higher HER activity. In addition, the HER activity of Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC is consistently superior to Ru-SAs $@$ N-TC in both $0.5\textbf{M}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and $1\textbf{M}$ phosphate buffer solutions (PBS) (Figure  4c; and Figure S13, Supporting Information), illustrating the enhanced HER activities are not only contributed from the favorable HER kinetics, but intrinsic electrocatalytic activity should be also taken into consideration. Therefore, mass activities in $1\ \mathrm{~M~}\ \mathrm{KOH}$ of the samples were calculated with respect to their mass loading of active metal species. As shown in Figure 4d, mass activities of the two Ru-containing catalysts were much higher than that of $20\%$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst, regardless of their higher overpotentials. At $0.1~\mathrm{V},$ the mass activities for Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC and Ru$\mathtt{S A s@N.T C}$ are 8.27 and $2.76\mathrm{~A~mg^{-1}}$ , respectively, about 17.6 and 5.9 times higher than $20\%$ Pt/C catalyst, indicating the improved atomic utilization efficiency of ${\mathrm{Ru-NPs/SAs@N-TC}}$ .
For further illustrating the high atomic utilization efficiency, Ru contents versus the overpotential at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ in $1\ \mathrm{{w}\ K O H}$ were plotted by Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC and some representative Ru/semiconductor composite electrocatalysts (Figure S14, Supporting Information). It is seen that even though the $\mathrm{{Ru-NPs}/}$ SAs $@$ N-TC performs a moderate HER activity, its Ru content is much lower than most of these catalysts, which validates its high intrinsic activity. The electrocatalytic active surface areas (ECSAs) were estimated by measuring the electrochemical double-layer capacitances $(C_{\mathrm{dl}})$ to explain the origin of different HER performance between the two Ru-containing catalysts (Figure  4e; and Figure S15, Supporting Information). Specifically, $\mathrm{Ru-NPs}/$ $\operatorname{SAs}@\operatorname{N-TC}$ exhibits the largest $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ of $37.5~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{-2}$ in $1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}$ , implying its more exposed active sites in good consistence with its BET data. The intrinsic HER catalytic activities of $\mathrm{{Ru-NPs}/}$ $\operatorname{SAs}@\operatorname{N-TC}$ and $\mathrm{Ru-SAs@N-T0}$ C were calculated by their turnover frequencies (TOF) in comparison with some other different reported electrocatalysts in $1\mathrm{~m~}$ KOH. As exhibited in Figure 4f, the TOF values of $\mathrm{Ru-NPs/SAs@N-TC}$ at the overpotentials of 50 and $100~\mathrm{{mV}}$ are 1.37 and 4.40 $\mathrm{H}_{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , respectively, which are larger than Ru-SAs $\textstyle({\widehat{a}})\mathrm{{N}}\cdot\mathrm{{TC}}$ and comparable to other reported elec trocatalysts.[7a,b,30] Electrocatalytic stability tests were measured by cyclic voltammetry (CV) and chronoamperometry in $1\textbf{M}$ KOH, $0.5\textbf{M}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ , and $1\mathrm{~M~}\mathrm{PBS}$ solutions, respectively. It is seen in Figure S16 (Supporting Information) that Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC was quite stable in both $1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}$ and $1\mathrm{~M~}\mathrm{PBS}$ solutions that there was almost no change observed in the current density after 50 000 s long-term electrolysis, but an obvious decay occurred in $0.5\textbf{M}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution due to the possible dissolution of Ru species in the acidic medium.[31] The polarization curves before and after 1000 cycles in the three media showed that only slight shifts toward higher overpotentials were observed in a wide range of $\mathrm{\pH}$ values. As seen in the HAADF-STEM image (Figure S17, Supporting Information), large Ru bulk was hardly found after 50 000 s long-term electrolysis in $1\mathbf{M}$ KOH, suggesting N-TC well supported Ru species and helped keep them from aggregation or Ostwald ripening during a sustained electrocatalytic process.
It is reported that Ru species are with advanced atomic configurations and inherent high work function, and they could be served as cocatalyst to boost photoexcited charge transfer and separation in photocatalysis after forming superior $\mathrm{Ru}/$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ hybrid catalysts.[32] Therefore, photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution was then conducted as a model reaction to further examine the cocatalyst effects of Ru species in our samples. Figure  5a shows the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production of pure N-TC, Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC, and Ru-SAs $@$ N-TC as well as $1\%$
Pt/N-TC (obtained by an in situ photodeposition). Note that pure N-TC exhibits negligible photocatalytic activity due to rapid recombination of photoinduced charge carriers, however, significant enhancement appears in Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC and $\operatorname{Ru-SAs}(\varnothing\operatorname{N-}$ TC despite of their low Ru loading amounts of 0.457 and $0.360~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively. Obviously, Ru species did work as cocatalyst for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution. In Figure 5b, Ru-NPs/SAs@N-TC shows a higher photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate of $100.0\upmu\mathrm{molh}^{-1}$ than Ru $\left.\mathbf{1}{\cdot}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}@\mathbf{N}{\cdot}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{C}\right.$ $(58.3\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ , even superior to that of $1\%$ Pt/N-TC $(83.9\ \upmu\mathrm{mol\h^{-1}})$ . The TOF value of ${\mathrm{Ru-NPs}}/{\mathrm{SAs}}{\ @\mathrm{N-TC}}$ is $110.4\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , which also surpassed $81.8\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ of Ru-SAs@N-TC (Table S4, Supporting Information). On the basis of the higher activity of ${\mathrm{Ru-NPs/SAs@N\mathrm{-}T C}}$ , pure Ru NPs supported sample (Ru-NPs@N-TC) was obtained and employed to further compare the photocatalytic/electrocatalytic HER performance under the same conditions. As clearly shown in Figure S18 (Supporting Information), the activities of $\operatorname{Ru-NPs}@\operatorname{N-TC}$ are commonly inferior to that of ${\mathrm{Ru-NPs}}/$ SAs $@$ N-TC and even that of Ru-SAs@N-TC, despite the higher Ru content of $0.851~\mathrm{wt\%}$ in Ru-NPs@N-TC (Table S1, Supporting Information). These results indeed demonstrated the optimized structural configuration of coexisted Ru NPs and SAs in Ru-NPs/SAs@N-TC could synergistically promote its electrocatalytic/photocatalytic performance by maximizing the atomic efficiency of Ru species. In addition, these results should be served as the indirect evidences to support the existence of Ru SAs in Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC.
However, the photoexcited charge transfer and separation efficiency of Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC was inferior to ${\mathrm{Ru-SAs@N}}.$ - TC catalyst (Figures S19 and S20, Supporting Information). The unusual photoelectric efficiency of Ru-SAs@N-TC might be attributed to its higher concentration of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{\nu}$ defects created by Ru SAs, which could accelerate the transfer and separation of photogenerated charge carriers. However on the other hand, the only optimization of charge separation efficiency is a necessary condition but not sufficient for promoting photocatalytic performance.[33] The photocatalytic stabilities of the two Ru-containing catalysts were then evaluated by three cyclings under the same condition. As shown in Figure  5c, Ru-NPs/ $\mathtt{S A s@N\mathrm{-}T C}$ and ${\mathrm{Ru-SAs@N-TC}}$ are quite stable under successive photocatalytic reactions.
As a proof of concept, photoassisted electrocatalytic HER was further employed to evaluate the two Ru-containing catalysts due to their effective structural configuration and intrinsic high activity. The tests were performed in $1\mathrm{~M~KOH}$ by careful removal of heat effects during reaction. Figure 5d shows the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves that the decrease in overpotential occurred for both Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC and Ru-SAs $@$ N-TC by the aid of photoirradiation, indicating the photogenerated charges did contribute to electrocatalytic HER to some extent. The decrease $(\Delta E)$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ in overpotential are 21 and $25~\mathrm{mV}$ for ${\mathrm{Ru-NPs/SAs@N\mathrm{-}T C}}$ and R ${\mathrm{u}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{SAs@N}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TC}}$ catalysts, respectively. This enhancement by photoassistance confirms the synergistic contribution of photocatalysis and electrocatalysis for HER process, despite the effect is not very vigorous.
Comprehensively, the two Ru-containing catalysts endowed promising and function-integrated activities in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution driven by either electrocatalysis or photocatalysis or even both together. Impressively, the performance of Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC is always superior to ${\mathrm{Ru-SAs@N-TC}}$ , ascribed to multiple effects of its structural configuration. First, the electronic structure of atomically dispersed Ru species could be manipulated by the surrounding Ru NPs, thus the water adsorption ability might be changed to facilitate water dissociation.[9a] As a result, the more active Ru species would participate in the HER process, specifically reflected by the consistently higher TOF values of Ru-NPs/SAs@N-TC. Second, difference in energylevel structure between Ru-NPs/SAs@N-TC and ${\mathrm{Ru-SAs@N}}.$ TC plays the key role in determining the charge transfer and separation efficiency during catalytic processes. As shown in Figure S21 (Supporting Information), the Mott–Schottky plots of N-TC, Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC, and $\operatorname{Ru-SAs@N-TC}$ appear as a distinct positive slope. This is a typical n-type semiconductor behavior. The flat-band potentials $(E_{\mathrm{fb}})$ were estimated by extrapolating the plots to $1/\mathsf C^{2}=0$ , essentially similar to the conduction band (CB) position of n-type semiconductor.[34] The positive shifts of $E_{\mathrm{fb}}$ occurred in the presence of Ru species for Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC and $R\mathbf{u}{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}@\mathbf{N}{\cdot}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{C}$ , which signified a decrease in bending of band edge, thereby benefiting to charge transfer in thermodynamics.[35] Noticeably, a larger $E_{\mathrm{fb}}$ shift of ${\mathrm{Ru-NPs}}/{\mathrm{SAs}}{\ @\mathrm{N-TC}}$ $(-0.23\mathrm{~V},$ vs $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}]$ than ${\mathrm{Ru-SAs@N}}$ - TC $(-0.36~\mathrm{V},$ vs $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}_{\mathrm{\ell}}^{\mathrm{\prime}}$ ) could be discerned, so it has smaller bending degree of its CB and thus thermodynamically favorable electron transfer.[36] Besides, the formed Schottky junction at the interfaces of $\mathrm{Ru}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ would also accelerate charge flow because of the relatively lower metal work function of Ru than that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Electrons will transfer from Ru to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ across the interface of the Schottky barrier and form a new Fermi level.
This change should be resulted from their electronic interaction at the interface, uncovered by XPS results. Moreover, the charge-carrier density of the varied Ru species supported catalysts could be compared by the slope of the Mott–Schottky plot because of their inverse relationship, i.e., the smaller slope implies a higher charge-carrier density. Interestingly, Ru$\mathsf{S A s@N–T C}$ exhibited the smallest slope with a higher density of charge-carrier, indicating its higher efficiency of electron transfer and intrinsic electrical conductivity in accordance with its electrochemical impedance spectroscopy and photocurrent results (Figure S19, Supporting Information). Therefore, the higher catalytic performance of Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC might be attributed to a balance between thermodynamics and kinetics. In addition, the larger specific surface area $(307.5~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ of Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC should be also in favor of mass transfer and improving its catalytic HER kinetics.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have prepared two kinds of catalysts of ${\mathrm{Ru-NPs/SAs@N-TC}}$ and Ru-SAs $@$ N-TC by utilizing $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}$ - incorporated $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 as precursor via a facile pyrolysis process. The dispersion states of Ru species were able to be indirectly regulated just by varying the initial feeding amount of $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}$ ions. As a result, the Ru NPs and SAs coexisted sample of Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC exhibits much higher intrinsic activity compared to Ru-SAs@N-TC either in electrocatalytic or photocatalytic HER.
Typically, it delivers a lower overpotential of $97~\mathrm{\mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and a smaller Tafel slop of $58~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ than that of Ru-SAs $@$ N-TC for electrocatalytic HER in $1\textbf{M}$ KOH. Also, the rate of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution of ${\mathrm{Ru-NPs}}/{\mathrm{SAs}}\ @{\mathrm{N-TC}}$ reaches to $100.0\upmu\mathrm{molh}^{-1}$ under 300 W Xe lamp irradiation, even superior to the control sample of $1\%$ Pt/N-TC. Their activity could be further optimized by integrating both electrocatalytic and photocatalytic processes in one system. The good catalytic performance of Ru-NPs/SAs $@$ N-TC should be related to its optimal intrinsic activity boosted by synergistic coupling of Ru NPs and SAs. The results have paved a new way for delicately designing high-efficiency and dual-functional catalyst by stabilization of MOFs, but it needs furthermore precise control on atomic coordination environments and coordination numbers of $\mathtt{R u}$ species that are very important for catalysis.
## 4. Experimental Section
All the chemicals used in the present study were analytical grade and used without further purification. Deionized $H_{2}O$ was used throughout the experiments.
Synthesis of $R u^{3+}/N H_{2}$ -MIL-125 Precursors: Typically, a certain amount of anhydrous ${\sf R u C l}_{3}$ and 3.75  mmol of 2-aminoterephthalic acid were dissolved in a mixed solution of N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) $(45.0\ m L)$ ) and $C H_{3}O H$ $(5.0~\mathsf{m L})$ with magnetic stirring for $15m i n$ . After that, Titanium (IV) isopropoxide (TPOT, 2.5  mmol, $0.75~\mathrm{mL}$ ) was quickly injected into the aforementioned mixture with magnetic stirring for another 15  min, followed by solvothermal treatment for $72\mathrm{~h~}$ at ${150~^{\circ}C}.$ . The resultant products were centrifuged at 7000 rpm for 6 min and washed with DMF and ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{O H}}$ three times and then dried under vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ Wherein, the varied initial feeding molar ratio of ${\mathsf{R u C l}}_{3}/$ TPOT $(\mathsf{R u}/\mathsf{T i})$ and pristine ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ -MIL-125 precursors were, prepared using the same synthetic procedures by changing the amounts of ${\sf R u C l}_{3}$ .
Preparation of N-TC, Ru-NPs/SAs@N-TC, and $R u-S A s@N-T C$ : The MOF-derived N-TC, Ru-NPs/SAs $\textcircled{0}$ N-TC, and Ru-SAs@N-TC samples were prepared by pyrolysis of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ -MIL-125, $2\%$ ${\mathsf{R u}}^{3+}/{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ -MIL-125, and $5\%$ $\S{\sf R u}^{3+}/{\sf N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 precursors at $800~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ with a heating rate of $2{\mathrm{~}}^{\circ}C{\mathrm{~}}{\mathsf{m i n}}^{-1}$ under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere for $3h$ , respectively.
Preparation of Ru-NPs@N-TC: Ru-NPs $@$ N-TC was obtained by an impregnation-reduction method. Typically, $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{700}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{m g}}$ of N-TC was dispersed in $700~\mathsf{m L}$ mixed solution of deionized water and ethanol (v/v, 1:1) by sonication for $30\mathrm{\min}$ . Then, $0.2~\mathsf{m L}$ of ${\sf R u C l}_{3}$ solution $(70\ m g\ m L^{-1}),$ ) was added in the suspension and stirring for $2h$ . After that, the powder was centrifuged, washed with water and ethanol three times and then redispersed in $700~\mathsf{m L}$ mixed solution of deionized water and ethanol (v/v, 1:1). Subsequently, $70~\mathsf{m L}$ of $N a B H_{4}$ solution $(70\ m g\ m L^{-1})$ was dropped into the mixture and stirred for $2h$ . The resulting powder was collected, rinsed by deionized water and ethanol three times, and then dried under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}C$ .
Materials Characterization: The morphology, structure, and composition of the samples were characterized by field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM, Hitachi SU-8020), TEM/ HR-TEM (JEOL JEM-2100F), HAADF-STEM (Titan Cubed Themis G2 300 at $200~\mathsf{k V}.$ ) with an energy dispersive $\mathsf{X}$ -ray spectrometer, XRD (Bruker D8), XPS (Thermo Scientific Escalab 250Xi), FTIR (Nicolet iN10MX), and Raman spectra (LabRAM Hr800, $\lambda=532~{\mathsf{n m}}$ ). EPR was measured at room temperature with a Bruker A300-10 spectrometer. The TG–DSC curves were obtained on a Mettler-Toledo-Star system with a heating rate of $10^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ from room temperature to ${\mathsf{1}}100^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. Specific surface areas were determined by BET method using Micromeritics ASAP-2020 equipment. The content of Ru was determined by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (Agilent ICP-OES 730). The steady-state fluorescence (PL) spectra were detected at $300~\mathsf{n m}$ excitation wavelength.
Electrocatalytic HER Measurements: The electrocatalytic HER was performed in a standard three-electrode system controlled by a CHI 760E electrochemistry workstation. A graphite rod and saturated calomel electrode electrode were employed as the counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively. To prepare the working electrode, $4.0\ \mathrm{mg}$ of electrocatalyst and $40~\upmu\up L$ Nafion solution were dispersed in $960~\upmu\upiota$ ethanol by sonication at least $30\mathrm{\min}$ to form a homogeneous ink. Subsequently, $5\upmu\up L$ of the suspension was dropped onto a polished glassy carbon electrode with a diameter of $3\mathsf{m m}$ (surface area $0.071\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ loading amount ${\approx}0.282~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2},$ . The HER performances were elevated in ${\sf N}_{2}$ -saturated 1  m KOH, $0.5~\mathsf{\ m}$ ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ and $1M\ P B S$ solutions, respectively. Before measurement, CV was applied at a scan rate of $50m\vee s^{-1}$ for 40 cycles to obtain stable catalyst surfaces. Then, the polarization curves were recorded by LSV tests at a scan rate of $5~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ All polarization curves were corrected with $90\%$ IR-compensation. To estimate the ECSAs of catalysts, CV measurements were performed in nonfaradaic region with scan rate from 5 to $30\ m\vee s^{-1}$ .
Photocatalytic and Photoassisted Electrocatalytic HER: Photocatalytic HER was carried out in a top-irradiation-type Pyrex reaction cell connected to a closed gas circulation and evacuation system. $20~\mathsf{m g}$ of catalyst was suspended in $700~\mathrm{mL}$ of aqueous solution containing $20~\mathsf{m L}$ of methanol as a sacrificing agent, without an additional cocatalyst. The suspension was then thoroughly degassed and irradiated by a $300\ \forall\ \mathsf{X}\mathsf{e}$ lamp $(\lambda=320-780~\mathsf{n m}$ , MicroSolar 300, Perfectlight, China). The amounts of produced ${\sf H}_{2}$ were analyzed using an on-line gas chromatography. For photoassisted electrocatalytic HER in ${\mathsf{1~M}}{\mathsf{K O H}}$ , the 300 W Xe lamp was utilized as a UV–visible light source and positioned about $6c m$ away from working electrode. The LSV curves with light were recorded under constant light irradiation.
